# MCP Adoption Strategy

- Prefer Cursor native tools first for code search, shell, web research, and browser inspection.
- **Mandatory triggers** live in **`.cursor/rules/yum4less-agent-orchestration.mdc`** and **`AGENTS.md`** — use MCP when that table says MUST, not only when convenient.
- **Installed in this repo (`.cursor/mcp.json`):** read-only Postgres, official GitHub (Docker), Playwright (headless), Semgrep Guardian when the local Semgrep CLI is installed, and Context7 for current library documentation. Use them where they remove repetitive verification work; do not add more without explicit approval.
- Do not add MCP servers to Yum4Less just because they exist; add one only when it removes repetitive work tied to an external system.
- Keep the active MCP set small, ideally `3-5` servers max, and prefer read-only or least-privilege configurations first.

## Postgres MCP

- Read-only connection to local `yum4less_dev` on port `5433`; run `npm run db:up` before use.
- Use for schema inspection, seed/store verification, latest `price_observations` after weekly-ad ingest, provider cache snapshots, and debugging integration-test DB state.
- Prefer Vitest integration tests for merge-gating DB behavior; use Postgres MCP for exploratory evidence and agent verification, not as a substitute for automated tests.
- Do not treat MCP SQL as a write path; schema changes belong in `db/init/` migrations and application code.

## GitHub MCP

- Official `ghcr.io/github/github-mcp-server` via Docker; requires `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` in the environment (never commit it).
- Use for PR/issue queries, workflow run status, and failed CI check inspection during review and release readiness.
- Prefer `gh` CLI for creating PRs, pushing branches, and other mutating GitHub actions unless the user explicitly wants MCP for those.
- The deprecated npm package `@modelcontextprotocol/server-github` must not be used.

## Playwright MCP

- Use for repeatable browser verification of Yum4Less UI flows (ZIP search, recommendations, trust signals, map) when agents need evidence beyond Vitest; start `npm run dev` first and stay on localhost with seeded or fixture-backed data.
- Do not use Playwright MCP for production weekly-ad scraping or as a substitute for fast unit/integration tests.

## Semgrep Guardian MCP

- Use for security, dependency, and secrets scanning around agent-written code, PR review, and release-readiness checks.
- The project hook wrapper runs non-blocking local `semgrep scan` checks with explicit security/TypeScript configs after file edits and at stop; if Semgrep is unavailable or the hook reports an error, treat it as advisory setup/follow-up context, not a passed scan.
- Semgrep complements, but does not replace, `npm test`, route validation tests, response-sanitization checks, and Yum4Less trust/freshness review.

## Context7 MCP

- Fetches current, version-specific documentation for libraries and frameworks (Next.js, React, Playwright, `pg`, etc.) so agents do not rely on stale training data.
- Use during implementation when API shapes, imports, or framework patterns are uncertain; append `use context7` to prompts when doc freshness matters.
- Optional `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` from [context7.com/dashboard](https://context7.com/dashboard) improves rate limits; set as a user environment variable or in Cursor MCP settings — never commit keys into `.cursor/mcp.json`.
- Complements native web search; it does not replace Postgres, Playwright, or Semgrep verification gates.

## General

- Never commit `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN`, `CONTEXT7_API_KEY`, or other secrets into `.cursor/mcp.json`.
- Treat `Postman`, `Sentry`, and hosted browser MCPs as later-stage options driven by real workflow pain, not default setup.
- Avoid overlapping browser, filesystem, and shell MCPs unless there is a clear workflow gap that native tools do not cover.
